            <ArticleTitle>Transmitral diagnostic cardioscopy in a coronary artery bypass graft patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1353-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 69-year-old man with unstable angina and impaired left ventricular function who was admitted for emergency coronary artery bypass grafting had echocardiographic findings suggestive of a left ventricular thrombus. A transmital cardioscopy was successfully performed at surgery, without video assistance, to confirm the diagnosis. We discuss our approach and the advantages.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
